---
// Homepage.
//
// NOTE: 03-PAGE-SPECS has no file for "/". The sitemap carries 14 URLs and the specs
// cover 13, so the head tags and block order below were authored to the same standard as
// the spec'd pages rather than copied from one. Title 44 chars, meta 149.
import PageShell from '../layouts/PageShell.astro';
import { byPath, PAGES, CLUSTERS } from '../lib/pages.js';

const page = byPath('/');

const answer =
  'Plainly is an independent information resource for Dubai property owners, publishing ' +
  'valuation, handover and short-let guidance. It publishes what things cost and what the ' +
  'rules require: snagging runs AED 666–999 for a typical apartment, holiday home operators ' +
  'take 15–25% of gross, and the golden visa threshold is AED 2 million.';

const clusters = [
  {
    key: 'snagging',
    heading: 'Snagging and handover',
    blurb:
      'What an inspection costs, what the checklist covers, and the twelve-month window ' +
      'in which a developer still has to fix what it finds.',
  },
  {
    key: 'holiday',
    heading: 'Short-letting and holiday homes',
    blurb:
      'What operators charge, the DET permit you cannot legally let without, and how ' +
      'short-let actually compares with a long-term tenant once costs come out.',
  },
  {
    key: 'valuation',
    heading: 'Valuation',
    blurb:
      'How a Dubai valuation is produced, what each method costs, and the DLD-certified ' +
      'route the golden visa requires.',
  },
];
---

<PageShell page={page} h1="Property guidance for people who already own in Dubai" answer={answer}>
  <h2>What this site is</h2>

  <p>
    Plainly publishes advice, data and rules for people who own a property in Dubai, or are
    about to take handover of one. It does not advertise property, list units or quote asking
    prices — that is a licensed activity requiring a Trakheesi permit, and this site stays
    outside that category deliberately.
  </p>

  <p>
    The reason to read anything here is that the numbers are on the page. Most sites serving
    this market put pricing behind a contact form. Where a figure is published, it is
    reproduced here with the authority it came from and the date it was checked. Where one is
    not published, the page says so instead of guessing.
  </p>

  {
    clusters.map((c) => {
      const pillar = byPath(CLUSTERS[c.key].pillar);
      const children = PAGES.filter(
        (p) => p.cluster === c.key && p.path !== CLUSTERS[c.key].pillar,
      );
      return (
        <section>
          <h2>{c.heading}</h2>
          <p>{c.blurb}</p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <a href={pillar.path}>{pillar.crumb}</a>
            </li>
            {children.map((p) => (
              <li>
                <a href={p.path}>{p.crumb}</a>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </section>
      );
    })
  }

  <h2>Guides</h2>
  <ul>
    {
      PAGES.filter((p) => p.cluster === 'blog').map((p) => (
        <li>
          <a href={p.path}>{p.crumb}</a>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <h2>Who is behind it</h2>

  <p>
    Plainly is an information service. It is not a property brokerage, holds no RERA
    registration and never acts as the advertiser of record. Where a page describes a service
    that only a licensed firm can perform — a valuation for a sale, for instance — that work is
    carried out by a named licensed partner, and their registration number is shown on the page
    attributed to them.
  </p>

  <p>
    Plainly earns by referring readers to those licensed businesses. That is worth knowing
    before you read anything else here, which is why it is on the homepage rather than in a
    footer.
  </p>
</PageShell>
